{"sections": [{"spans": [{"text": "I took maybe five economics classes at Caltech as an undergrad between 2002 and 2006. In my experience there were two types:", "modifiers": {}}], "indent": 0, "quoted": false, "type": "plain", "is_rtl": false}, {"spans": [{"text": "Economics classes that were effectively math classes. During class, a professor would list some axioms and prove theorems on the board. Problem sets and tests would consist of more proofs. Occasionally there might be a story to explain where the axioms were coming from and otherwise motivate the theory, but most students, focused on passing the class, would quickly forget those, since what mattered to passing was the math. The axioms seemed to be chosen specifically to make the math elegant or reasonable, and whether they fit reality wasn’t even discussed.

Everyone at Caltech has to take calculus, linear algebra, and some other advanced math courses, so every economics class could assume that background and build on top of it. This was good because it allowed other classes to be taught at a higher level of rigor, but it was also bad in my opinion, because it led to a bias toward math and proofs, and away from fundamental concepts and away from applications.", "modifiers": {}}], "indent": 0, "quoted": false, "type": "ordered-list", "is_rtl": false}, {"spans": [{"text": "Economics classes that had what I thought was a good balance of math and social science. The math and models served to make these courses more rigorous, but the professor wouldn’t lose sight of the fact that the models were just models and might not always apply.

A course I remember from this category was Organizational Design by Colin Camerer. There was a lot of game theory and math to illustrate points and model scenarios out, but much of the course was spent discussing how and when the theory fit reality. I still use a lot of the concepts I first learned in that course in my day-to-day experience running Quora.", "modifiers": {}}], "indent": 0, "quoted": false, "type": "ordered-list", "is_rtl": false}]}
